# Constraints for the Vea board: LFE5U-12F-6BG256C, caBGA256, speed grade 6.
#
# The ball names, the bank numbers and the alternate functions come from the Lattice
# pinout file of this density, vea-board/fpga-ecp5_12u-pinout.csv, column CABGA256.
#
# The Project Trellis device database holds the same data, and it is the data that
# nextpnr-ecp5 itself uses. This command proves that the two agree, and it fails if a
# pinout of the wrong density or the wrong package is in use:
#
#   python3 vea-board/scripts/gen_ecp5_pinout.py --verify vea-board/fpga-ecp5_12u-pinout.csv
#
# Take a ball from the pinout of the density on the board. A pinout of the LFE5U-45F gives
# four PLLs, and this part has two.
#
# Every bank in use here needs VCCIO at 3.3 V for LVCMOS33.

# ---- Configuration -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The configuration mode pins select the boot source. CFGMDN[2:0] = 010 selects master
# SPI. The pins are dedicated, so they carry no LOCATE statement. Strap them on the PCB:
#
#   CFG_0 (N10) = 0    CFG_1 (P10) = 1    CFG_2 (R10) = 0
#
# This file does not set MASTER_SPI_PORT. That setting keeps the master SPI port alive as
# a configuration port in user mode, which the sysCONFIG user guide calls a Background
# Mode port. Its purpose is background self-reconfiguration. The design does not use it.
#
# COMPRESS_CONFIG makes the bitstream smaller, which shortens the boot time. nextpnr-ecp5
# and ecppack take COMPRESS_CONFIG and MCCLK_FREQ here. ecppack takes the width of the
# boot read with --spimode, and background reconfiguration with --background.
#
# Board resistors, from Table 6.2 of the hardware checklist (FPGA-TN-02038). Every
# pull-up goes to VCCIO8:
#
#   PROGRAMN  4.7 kOhm pull-up
#   INITN     4.7 kOhm pull-up
#   CSSPIN    4.7 kOhm to 10 kOhm pull-up. Put it near the flash.
#   MCLK/CCLK 510 Ohm to 1 kOhm pull-up, and a 22 Ohm to 80 Ohm series resistor near
#             the transmit side.
#   CFG[2:0]  1 kOhm to 10 kOhm pull-up for a 1. Tie to ground for a 0.
#
# The dedicated configuration balls, for the schematic:
#
#   PROGRAMN R9   INITN T9    DONE P9    CCLK/MCLK N9
#   TCK T10       TDI R11     TDO M10    TMS T11

SYSCONFIG COMPRESS_CONFIG=ON;

# ---- Boot flash, the master SPI port ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The boot flash holds the bitstream only. User logic does not use it. The user program
# is in a different SPI memory. See the program memory block below.
#
# Two kinds of SPI exist on this device, and they are not interchangeable:
#
#   1. The master SPI port, below. It boots the device, so its pins are fixed package
#      pins. No choice of ball exists. Table 6.3 of the hardware checklist (FPGA-TN-02038)
#      splits them in two groups for MSPI mode:
#        Shared:    MISO, MOSI, CSSPIN, DOUT for a 1-bit read. D[3:0] and CSSPIN for a
#                   quad read.
#        Dedicated: PROGRAMN, INITN, DONE, and the MCLK clock.
#
#   2. A SPI device on any other ball. The fabric drives it, so any free user I/O works.
#      The program memory below is such a device.
#
# Size: Table 4.1 of the sysCONFIG user guide (FPGA-TN-02039) gives 5.42 Mb for the
# largest uncompressed LFE5-12 bitstream. Lattice recommends an 8 Mb SPI flash.
#
# Programming: the JTAG port of the FPGA reaches this flash through the master SPI port.
# The FPGA needs no user design for this. See FPGA-TN-02050.
#
# Table 6.2 of the ECP5 Evaluation Board user guide (FPGA-EB-02017) shows the topology
# that Lattice uses. Its balls are for the LFE5UM5G-85F in caBGA381, not for this part:
#
#   CCLK_MCLK_SCK U3    DQ0_MOSI W2    DQ1_MISO V2
#   BUSY_CSSPIN   R2    DQ2      Y2    DQ3      W1
#
# The Dual Function column of the pinout file gives these balls. The Trellis database
# agrees with each one:
#
#   MCLK/CCLK N9   CCLK    dual MCLK/SCK. Dedicated.
#   CSSPIN    N8   PB15A   dual HOLDN/DI/BUSY/CSSPIN/CEN
#   D0/MOSI   T8   PB11B   dual D0/MOSI/IO0
#   D1/MISO   T7   PB11A   dual D1/MISO/IO1
#   D2        M7   PB9B    dual D2/IO2.  Quad read only.
#   D3        N7   PB9A    dual D3/IO3.  Quad read only.
#
# The design has no port on these balls, so this block has no LOCATE statement.

# ---- Clock ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# P6 is LLC_GPLL0T_IN, the reference input of the lower left PLL. The LFE5U-12F has two
# PLLs, and both sit in the lower corners. P11 (LRC_GPLL0T_IN) is the other one.

LOCATE COMP "clk_25mhz" SITE "P6";
IOBUF  PORT "clk_25mhz" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;
FREQUENCY PORT "clk_25mhz" 25 MHZ;

# ---- Program memory, external SPI memory -------------------------------------------------
#
# This device sits on usual fabric I/O, so these balls are a free choice. Move them to
# suit the layout. The four pins stay in one bank to keep the skew between them small.
#
# vea_mem_if sends a 24-bit address. A part with a 16-bit address reads the wrong bytes.
#
# Programming: an external SPI programmer writes this memory through a header on these
# four nets. Hold PROGRAMN low while it writes. The FPGA then stays in initialization,
# and its user I/O stay tristated. See sections 4.6.2 and 5.2 of the sysCONFIG user guide
# (FPGA-TN-02039).
#
# Put a pull-up on the chip select near the memory. Before configuration, the user I/O
# have a weak pull-down. Without the pull-up, the memory is selected during boot.

LOCATE COMP "prog_spi_sck"  SITE "M11";
LOCATE COMP "prog_spi_cs_n" SITE "M12";
LOCATE COMP "prog_spi_mosi" SITE "M13";
LOCATE COMP "prog_spi_miso" SITE "M14";

IOBUF PORT "prog_spi_sck"  IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
IOBUF PORT "prog_spi_cs_n" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
IOBUF PORT "prog_spi_mosi" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
IOBUF PORT "prog_spi_miso" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 PULLMODE=UP;

# ---- Status indicators -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Bit 4 is halt. Bits 3 to 0 are the illegal, unsupported, trap and unaligned errors.

LOCATE COMP "led_n[4]" SITE "C1";
LOCATE COMP "led_n[3]" SITE "C2";
LOCATE COMP "led_n[2]" SITE "C3";
LOCATE COMP "led_n[1]" SITE "D1";
LOCATE COMP "led_n[0]" SITE "D3";

IOBUF PORT "led_n[4]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
IOBUF PORT "led_n[3]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
IOBUF PORT "led_n[2]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
IOBUF PORT "led_n[1]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
IOBUF PORT "led_n[0]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33 DRIVE=8;
